-- [ ] **The audit and review payload consolidation** -- one canonical prompt per use case from the disposition list in [`surface-area-report.md`](../.agents/audits/surface-area-report.md): the handover-audit pair merged, `kelsey-code-reviewer`'s useful checks folded into `bash-audit.skill.md`, the two non-current Claude-format skills (`dhh-code-audit.skill.md`, `architecture-doc-reviewer.skill.md`) dropped, `toc.sh` relocated out of skills, and `.agents/audits` dissolved while `.agents/drafts` gains a contract and is drained rather than dissolved. The folder is live workflow output (`/scope` writes a saved brief there), it is the only home of four unshipped prompts (`maintenance.md` here, and `sequential-work.md`, `parallel-work.md` and `task-queue.md`, which M3.2.3 ships), and it holds `command-surface-matrix/`, a skill filed in the wrong folder. What the drain removes is the superseded brief, the spent braindump and the misfiled skill, not the folder. For the documentation-audit use case, one pi-native prompt per [`documentation-audit-comparison.md`](../.agents/audits/documentation-audit-comparison.md), absorbing `architecture-doc-reviewer.skill.md`. Ends when no item of the disposition list survives. Nature of change: prompt and skill edits, deletions and moves.
-- [ ] **The review-pass framing fixes** -- three, tracking the `2026-09-18` [AGENT_FEEDBACK](AGENT_FEEDBACK.md) entries: round-cap and blocker re-review guidance for correctness reviews, the base commit and exact diff range named in a review directive, and a seeded/runtime copy pair named by full path with the authoritative copy stated.
-- [ ] **The review-pass mutation step** -- the pass's required mutation over the changed behaviour, so a review that changes behaviour without a mutation over it does not close.
-- [ ] **Retire the reasoning-only assertion audit** -- the audit that covered assertions alone, superseded by the mutation step and the test-quality campaign.
-- [ ] **Adopt the churn survey and the fan-out protocol as standing passes** -- with their triggers and frequency, and the bounded loop in [`review-loop-run.md`](../.agents/drafts/review-loop-run.md) as the first consumer of that review shape.
-- [ ] **The exhaustive consistency pass** -- a `-run`-family prompt that sweeps, once and exhaustively at the end of an iteration or a run, the classes that burn the late review rounds: documents that pin output strings, commands, exit semantics or count-and-shape contracts the change altered; one term spelled several ways across prompts, policies and records; counts repeated in records. The judgement classes stay with the reviewer.
-- [x] **One dispatch surface for the worker and the reviewer** -- the tree carries two launch mechanisms for one job: [`scripts/dispatch.sh`](../scripts/dispatch.sh) for a worker, and the fixed runner `review-dispatch.sh` for a two-seat review, which the launch-site gate allowlists as the one fixed run. The split exists because the entrypoint cannot express a seat count, while the reserved terms already define one: [`terminology.md`](../docs/reference/terminology.md) `## role` says a request names a role and the number of distinct models it needs, that seat n takes entry n, and that a seat is never filled by duplicating a model. The runner instead runs two seats of one assignment. Resolved on operator direction, 2026-10-23: given n seats and m candidates, where the candidates are the role line's entries filtered to those that can be attempted now, seat i takes candidate ((i - 1) mod m) + 1, so the seating is one rotation over the reachable list. A retryable outcome retries the seat's own assignment, and an unretryable one drops that candidate and moves the seat to the next in the remaining rotation, wrapping to the first when the failed one was last. Seats fire concurrently within a turn and are read at the turn boundary, so a turn costs its slowest seat rather than the sum, and the candidate set has one writer per turn. Anything finer than a turn boundary is out of scope for this mechanism: it belongs to the **M3.6.6 - The scheduler** sub-milestone, which is filed unscoped and would subsume both this mechanism and the task queue. [`terminology.md`](../docs/reference/terminology.md) `## role` and `## role line` now state that rule. Retiring the runner into the entrypoint means building the seat path the terms describe and correcting the two records that still describe a repeat: the shipped reviewer section, and the role-table ADR's rejected-alternative line that says one line cannot serve two seats. [`dispatch.md`](../docs/reference/dispatch.md) names no seat today, because the mechanism has none. The names go with it: `pi-reviewer-dispatch` survives in the installed agent home and in one roadmap example list, and `opencode-dispatch` survives in closed rows and settled records. Filed by the dispatch-ownership run's follow-through, handover `20261023-25`. Nature of change: a seat-count resolution path, the reviewer runner and its allowlist entry deleted, its test ported, and every carrier retargeted. Closed 2026-10-23: the entrypoint runs seats (`--seats <n> --run`) over the role's routable candidates, so a review names `_REVIEWER` and a seat count and the seating is the rotation the terms describe. `review-dispatch.sh`, its allowlist entry and `tests/test_review_dispatch.sh` are deleted; its checks are carried where the seat path can hold them (a dirty tree is refused before the first attempt, and a seat that logs nothing stops the run), its model-binding check retires because the entrypoint passes the full id from the table, and the shipped `## Running Review Subagents` section now states the seat invocation. Handover `20261023-30`.
-- [ ] **Reviewer subagent as the compliance counterparty for gateless closes** -- a close without an operator acceptance gate (autonomous dispatch, fan-out merge-backs, units packaged for `/rebase`) has no counterparty to the closing agent's own framing, which is the failure class the 2026-10-02 rescope recorded: the agent believed its own six-slice framing, so a self-check by the same agent cannot catch it. Fix: a review subagent per the `_REVIEWER` role recommendation reviews the closed unit, and its check scope explicitly includes the unit's commit series against the type table and the one-commit-per-handover rule, alongside the existing review dimensions. Covers gateless closes and the as-is units `/rebase` applies -- the packaging step cannot check anything, so compliance must be checked before packaging. One of its checks is the scope rule: the unit's commit range matches the unit's scope -- one work unit, one commit -- the same test the `/wrapup` self-check runs for operator-gated closes. Raised (operator + agent, 2026-10-02). Refinement is slated for this sub-milestone.
-- [ ] **The document-maintenance review becomes executable** -- the register is now the `doc-maintenance` skill: the review method (claim extraction, the implementation cross-reference, the link-resolution step, and the cross-document consistency pass) is its `## Procedure`, and the diagnostic checklists are its `## The diagnostic register`. Two things the old register could not do are now stated as steps rather than as advice: a link is followed and checked against what the citing line claims, and one fact stated in two documents is one finding that names both sites. The rows below stay open for `/document` own remainder. Handover `20261006-09`.
