+- [ ] **The audit and review payload consolidation** -- the two folders' disposition discharged, from [`surface-area-report.md`](../.agents/audits/surface-area-report.md): the handover-audit pair merged, `kelsey-code-reviewer`'s useful checks folded into `bash-audit.skill.md`, the two non-current Claude-format skills (`dhh-code-audit.skill.md`, `architecture-doc-reviewer.skill.md`) dropped, `toc.sh` relocated out of skills, and `.agents/audits` dissolved while `.agents/drafts` gains a contract and is drained rather than dissolved. The folder is live workflow output (`/scope` writes a saved brief there), it is the only home of four unshipped prompts (`maintenance.md` here, and `sequential-work.md`, `parallel-work.md` and `task-queue.md`, which M3.2.3 ships), and it holds `command-surface-matrix/`, a skill filed in the wrong folder. What the drain removes is the superseded brief, the spent braindump and the misfiled skill, not the folder. Ends when no item of the disposition list survives. Nature of change: prompt and skill edits, deletions and moves.
+  - [ ] **The documentation-audit prompt** -- one pi-native prompt per [`documentation-audit-comparison.md`](../.agents/audits/documentation-audit-comparison.md), absorbing `architecture-doc-reviewer.skill.md` and the `documentation-pass` checklist the `doc-maintenance` skill already carries.
+  - [x] **The instruction-cleanup procedure** -- [`/instruction-cleanup`](../workflow/coding-agent/prompts/instruction-cleanup.md): one pass over one surface. It inventories the surface, categorises its instructions into runbooks by use case, deconflicts by subject, and puts every candidate in front of the operator one screen at a time, as a proposal, a flag or a rejected line. Its four faults are the harmful instruction, which covers a harmful clause inside a sound rule, the redundant instruction, ambiguous wording and ambiguous procedure. It measures nothing and runs no prompt, because the corpus must be deconflicted before any measurement can attribute a finding to a file. Its rewrites are held to `communication-principle-rewrite-syntax-never-semantics`, which the pass inlined into the shipped `AGENTS.md`. Every fold in this milestone runs through it. Closed 2026-10-25: the procedure is stated once and carries no open question, the four faults are one vocabulary with a flag route for the fault the agent cannot name yet, and the provenance register states what a record proves and what it does not. Handover `20261025-01`.
+  - [ ] **The corpus census over every review-instruction carrier** -- one pass over everything the cleanup pass touches, instead of a file at a time, because a single-file read cannot see that a rule it is reading is stated again elsewhere: that is why the comparison's two arms tied at twenty faults each and both missed `bash-audit.skill.md` holding the same rules 29 lines away. The set is six surfaces in `.agents/audits/` -- `kelsey-code-reviewer` (158 lines, bash and container), `architecture-doc-reviewer` (166, prose and docs), `dhh-code-audit` (99, Ruby and JavaScript, which this repository has neither of), `bash-audit` (29, already in pointer form), `test-quality-campaign` (106) and `test-assertion-sweep-brief` (49, report-only) -- together with the two cleanup prompts, which are instruction surfaces here too. `documentation-audit-comparison` and `surface-area-report` are excluded: they are settled records, not instructions. The fault lists are raised by the two prompts run over the set, not by the agent reading the files: the agent contributes the mechanical part, the ownership lookup and the fate table, and the operator rules. Delivered as one table per subject cluster: the carriers, the owner of the rules, the detector, and each carrier's fate. The rulings are the ground truth the eval grades against, which is why this runs first. Evidence: [`20261026-report-settled-instruction_cleanup_comparison.md`](../devlog/discussions/20261026-report-settled-instruction_cleanup_comparison.md). Handover `20261026-04`.
+  - [ ] **The consolidation, run once over the whole set** -- per subject cluster, one owner for the rules and one runbook carrying a pattern list that cites that owner rather than restating it. `code-review`'s general plane first, since two carriers restate the same feedback discipline and the same five output sections and the shell owns that plane once, and the general code-quality rules have no home at all today, `docs/development/conventions.md` being a single heading; then `code-review-bash`, over `bash-coding-conventions.md`'s 20 sections and `bash-audit`'s nine tags; container second, which no convention document owns today and which is the whole net-new residue of `kelsey-code-reviewer`, kept a consolidation target because the subject exists here even though its rules have no owner: `src/reasoning/base.dockerfile`, the provider images, and `src/build/image.sh`; and the unowned tail of the bash half, since `git status --porcelain` and `git diff` accumulation appear in no convention document and cannot die by citation, so they become new pattern rows or a new convention section; prose and docs third, leaning on `prose-review` and the documentation-audit prompt that does not exist. Every carrier the census names takes its fate in this same unit -- absorbed, absorbed-with-pointer, or deleted -- so no file is left half-folded. Depends on the census. Handover `20261026-04`.
+  - [ ] **The instruction-cleanup eval case set, saved and re-runnable** -- the item under test is the cleanup prompt; the test cases are the affected files; the ground truth is the census rulings, because grading against the goal-only draft's own output would make the draft the answer key and would ask whether the prompt matches a prompt rather than whether it raises valid faults. Three arms, all of them the item under test: `A`, `B`, and `A` minus the lines the census rules harmful -- the third tests the hypothesis that the long pass suppresses its own good instructions with its bad ones. One JSON per case in `workflow/coding-agent/skills/eval/evals/`, shaped like `commit-body-budget.json`, with `configuration_variable` naming which prompt text a run reads. Each case runs in a directory that holds only its carrier and the files the case names: the carriers sit in `.agents/audits/` and the rulings they are graded against sit in `devlog/`, so a case that can read the tree around it reads the answer key, and thirty of thirty-five cells of the first ablation run were contaminated that way. A saved case set is what turns every later edit to either prompt into a re-run over this file set instead of a fresh sample. Depends on the census for its assertions. Handover `20261026-04`.
+  - [ ] **Whether a review runbook carries a persona** -- the observation behind this is worth keeping: a persona activates judgement the rules do not state, the code-writing opinions and cross-domain experience of whoever it names. This corpus cannot settle it and should not pretend to: the effect would show up in a review's output, while the cleanup eval reads a fault list, so testing it needs a different case set with a review as its subject. `code-review-bash` ships rule-based, and a persona stance is added when a case set can measure it. Handover `20261026-04`.
+  - [ ] **A repository gate for the census instrument** -- dormant, unfiled by this pass: the census is a `.scratchpad/` sheet from a throwaway script and no gate checks it. It waits on a second instruction surface censused with the same instrument, and that second surface is the trigger rather than a date, because one surface cannot show an instrument generalises.
