 #### T6 - Performance Optimizations / UI Tweaks
 
+The pre-snapshot gate and the session identity export moved to [M3.4.2](#m342---environment-declaration-seam), and the structured task log to [M3.4.3](#m343---persistence-contract).
+
 - [x] **Session save suppressed by the autosave baseline** -- session export read its no-op baseline from the autosave dir, so a current autosave suppressed the durable exit record. The baseline now points at the durable branch point and the coupling is pinned by a test. Handover `20260921-06`.
-- [ ] **Pre-snapshot validation gate** -- configurable per-project check run by `start_agent.sh` before building `.agent-input/`; fail fast before the container starts
-- [ ] **Structured task logs** -- store structured logs per agent and task run (overlaps the T5 generated-artifact storage task)
-- [ ] **Session identity in the agent container environment** -- the `agent` service in `src/build/docker-compose.yml` passes only the four workspace variables, so a caller inside the agent container has no shell-level way to read which session it is running in. The `sandbox` service already receives `SESSION_ID`, `SESSION_TS`, `HOST_HEAD_SHA`, `SANITIZED_HOST_BRANCH`, and `RESET_VOLUME` from the same exported environment; the agent service does not, and the gap is one environment block. Export the identity variables into the agent service environment so the identity is answerable with one command, with no prefix and no namespace wrapper: the agent container runs a single service, so a bare key is unambiguous and a prefix adds a word the reader must strip mentally. Pin the agent service key set with a test, because the compose block that carries it is per-service and a later edit to the sibling service does not touch it. Raised by the operator (2026-09-30).
 - [ ] **Harness env keys enter the agent namespace unprefixed** -- the harness writes its own variables (`SESSION_ID`, `SESSION_TS`, `HOST_HEAD_SHA`, `SESSION_STATE`, and the `DIR` family) into an environment the provider also owns, under bare names that no prefix ties back to the harness. A reader in that container sees `SESSION_ID` and cannot tell from the name which layer set it. Two facts keep this recorded rather than acted on. No collision exists today: the provider sets no variable of the same name, so the two namespaces do not currently overlap. The agent container runs one service, so a bare key is never ambiguous within it. Operator ruling (2026-09-30): the agent environment is the agent's own namespace and stays pristine, so no harness prefix is introduced. The row records the convention and its stated limit -- the guarantee is single-service, so a second service or a future provider variable forces the decision again.
 
 #### T7 - Harness Specialization / Sandbox Persistence
 
-The skill-maintenance rows (backlog triage, fork-and-strip) moved to [M3.3.3](#m333---the-skill-maintenance-pipeline).
+The skill-maintenance rows (backlog triage, fork-and-strip) moved to [M3.3.3](#m333---the-skill-maintenance-pipeline). The environment-change persistence row moved to [M3.4.3](#m343---persistence-contract).
 
 - [ ] **Git boundary and repository state** -- one row for the git-related defects, in four parts, because they share one undefined boundary: **who may run git** (six modules run git without belonging to the git layer, and the three-valued dirty read is implemented twice); **the lock discipline** (what the harness's own `.git/index.lock` means, which reads set `GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0`, and the rule that only the diff pipeline may leave a deliberate lock); **the pipeline's ownership** (one artifact contract behind the three writers, and one owner each for the output directory and the index); and **the workflow state chain** (the apply/draft/confirm/reject states, and the composition rule that apply and draft are alternative consumers of one bundle, never a sequence). Source: [`20260925-design-draft-git_boundary.md`](../devlog/discussions/archive/20260925-design-draft-git_boundary.md), which subsumes [`20260924-design-draft-diff_pipeline_unification.md`](../devlog/discussions/archive/20260924-design-draft-diff_pipeline_unification.md) and [`20260925-design-draft-diff_workflow_invariants.md`](../devlog/discussions/archive/20260925-design-draft-diff_workflow_invariants.md).
 - [ ] **The session scratch depends on the project's ignore rule** -- `.scratchpad/` is ignored in this repository by its own `.gitignore`, and nothing writes `.git/info/exclude`, so in a delivered project whose ignore file lacks the rule the agent's scratch shows as untracked files and a `git add -A` can carry it into a commit. The harness creates the sandbox repository, so the rule can be written into the untracked exclude file at seed time, which no diff carries and no project has to cooperate with. Measured 2026-10-23: `findmnt` reports `.scratchpad/` inside the named volume `...-sandbox-data`, so the location is durable and only the ignore rule is missing. Nature of change: the seed path and its test.
 - [ ] **A worker's write capability is not a property of the worker** -- a read-only worker can write, because the dispatch scripts launch a write-enabled CLI in the caller's working tree with shell access, and nothing about the run distinguishes a reviewer from an implementer. The measured instance: a review worker ran its experiments inside the repository, reset the branch to unwind them, and removed the repository's local git identity. Guarding this per brief is a governance lapse rather than a control, because the guarantee would rest on the caller remembering a line. Two symptoms are already recorded and are not separate rows: any git operation that touches the index or the working tree reverts uncommitted session work, and a run directory inside the work tree is destroyed by `git clean -fdx`, taking the run's only evidence with it. The design question is what a worker's write capability is a property of, and what write mechanism several workers use safely at once. Nature of change: design, then a capability boundary and its checks.
 - [ ] **Commit-metadata capture** -- capture agent_id, task_id, timestamp with each commit; prerequisite for trusting autonomous output and for the M4.1 metadata-seeding milestone
-- [ ] **Environment-change persistence (install layers across runs)** -- apt installs, `pi update --self`, and `pi install` land in the per-run writable layer and tear down at run end; the copy/bind-mount model persists only mounted sources. Parked as not-in-scope for the current model (`roadmap_future.md` Deferred); revisit whether a persisted install-cache volume (durable-by-designation) is now worthwhile.
-- [ ] **Expose a capacity signal to the agent** -- the agent reported running out of room with no signal to read, because the environment exposes none. Expose remaining context or run budget so a capacity claim can be checked rather than inferred. A `pi` bump may supply it. Moved from M3.2.1 to the harness-persistence track, then to T12 with the pi-bump rows, then back here when that row was compacted (2026-10-04). Raised by `[A] 2026-10-02` in `devlog/AGENT_FEEDBACK.md`.
+- [ ] **Expose a capacity signal to the agent** -- [M3.6.2](#m362---measure) owns it; that milestone's **The capacity signal** row carries the work and the resolution of this one. Raised by `[A] 2026-10-02` in [AGENT_FEEDBACK](AGENT_FEEDBACK.md).
 - [ ] **Functional-stack navigation for questioning** -- a harness/behavioral design to keep a working "call stack" of the task context: the agent tracks the stack, emits keyword hints, and exposes navigation functions (abandon -> unwind, continue at same depth, resolve current, skip/drop current) when an operator question spawns. Resolves the multi-question-turns / implicit-acceptance grill-me family. Design proposal.
 
